  "lessons": [
    {
      "domain_id": "cloud-arch",
      "title": "Cloud Architecture \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Shared responsibility splits at the hypervisor for IaaS: the provider secures facilities, hardware, and virtualization; you own the OS, middleware, apps, and data.",
        "IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS: the line moves up the stack with PaaS and SaaS, but IaaS guests are always the customer's job.",
        "Auto-scaling matches capacity to demand automatically, and the load balancer spreads traffic across the fleet.",
        "Storage types: object storage is built for massive numbers of flat, immutable blobs (images, backups) with HTTP access; databases need low-latency block devices that look like local disks."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Worked example \u2014 shared responsibility\nSetup: The line moves up the stack with PaaS and SaaS, but IaaS guests are always the customer's job.\nReasoning: Shared responsibility splits at the hypervisor for IaaS: the provider secures facilities, hardware, and virtualization; you own the OS, middleware\u2026\nTakeaway: Object storage is built for massive numbers of flat, immutable blobs (images, backups) with HTTP access; databases need low-latency block devices\u2026",
        "walkthrough": "Read the setup and name the principle at play before looking at the reasoning. Then compare your answer with the takeaway \u2014 if they differ, re-read the concepts above and try the drill scenarios for this domain."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: shared means neither side.",
        "Common trap: refactor to cloud-native first, deadline or not.",
        "Common trap: pay the bill and hope next month is better.",
        "Common trap: buy a bigger discount plan and change nothing."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "linux-admin",
      "title": "Linux Administration \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "systemd: guessing with reinstalls wastes the evidence systemd already collected.",
        "journalctl: systemctl status shows the state and the most recent log lines; journalctl -u gives the full story, usually a config syntax error from yesterday's edit.",
        "Principle: 600 gives read/write to the owner only; the web server runs as a different user and needs read on files plus read+execute on directories to traverse them."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "$ ls -l /var/www/html/index.html\n-rw------- 1 deploy deploy 1832 Oct 3 09:00 index.html",
        "walkthrough": "600 gives read/write to the owner only; the web server runs as a different user and needs read on files plus read+execute on directories to traverse them. 644/755 is the standard; 777 hands write access to everyone \u2014 a vulnerability, not a fix."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: just change the SSH port and call it done.",
        "Common trap: use longer passwords and keep password auth.",
        "Common trap: disable GPG checking to make installs faster.",
        "Common trap: set everything to 777 so nothing is ever blocked."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "server-hw",
      "title": "Server Hardware \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "RAID 0 has no redundancy, and RAID 5's parity rebuilds on large disks are slow and risky \u2014 RAID 10 is the standard answer for performance + redundancy.",
        "ECC RAM corrects single-bit errors in hardware and logs them \u2014 which is why servers use it and desktops usually do not. POST memory errors localize the fault to a slot or stick.",
        "Hardware diagnostics: baseboard management controllers run independently of the host OS: they give KVM console, power cycle, and hardware sensors even when the OS is dead.",
        "Power redundancy: redundant hot-swappable PSUs exist so a failure is a maintenance event, not an outage \u2014 but redundancy is now gone, so the next failure is fatal."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Worked example \u2014 raid levels\nSetup: Baseboard management controllers run independently of the host OS: they give KVM console, power cycle, and hardware sensors even when the OS is dead.\nReasoning: It is the standard answer for lights-out remote sites \u2014 no BMC means a truck roll.\nTakeaway: RAID 0 has no redundancy, and RAID 5's parity rebuilds on large disks are slow and risky \u2014 RAID 10 is the standard answer for performance +\u2026",
        "walkthrough": "Read the setup and name the principle at play before looking at the reasoning. Then compare your answer with the takeaway \u2014 if they differ, re-read the concepts above and try the drill scenarios for this domain."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: power the server down first to be safe.",
        "Common trap: ask a non-technical employee to pull the plug.",
        "Common trap: ignore it; random crashes are normal.",
        "Common trap: open all the rack doors permanently."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "virtualization",
      "title": "Virtualization \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Snapshots vs backups: real backups are independent, restorable copies \u2014 ideally offsite and tested.",
        "Data protection: type 1 hypervisors (ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM) sit directly on hardware: lower overhead, better isolation, built for data centers.",
        "Containers vs VMs: live migration moves running VMs between hosts with no downtime, letting you evacuate a host for maintenance. Containers package the app with its dependencies but share the host OS kernel \u2014 no guest OS per instance, no hypervisor boot."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Worked example \u2014 snapshots vs backups\nSetup: Live migration moves running VMs between hosts with no downtime, letting you evacuate a host for maintenance.\nReasoning: Containers package the app with its dependencies but share the host OS kernel \u2014 no guest OS per instance, no hypervisor boot.\nTakeaway: In practice, sub-second starts and dense packing, ideal for elastic microservices.",
        "walkthrough": "Read the setup and name the principle at play before looking at the reasoning. Then compare your answer with the takeaway \u2014 if they differ, re-read the concepts above and try the drill scenarios for this domain."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: containers are just faster VMs with their own kernels.",
        "Common trap: they should delete old snapshots to save space and call it done.",
        "Common trap: snapshots are a complete backup strategy.",
        "Common trap: patch the host with VMs running on it."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "data",
      "title": "Data Concepts \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Data types: structured data fits relational tables; semi-structured (JSON logs) has tags but no fixed schema; unstructured (images, video) has no predefined model.",
        "Data quality: the transform stage exists to enforce quality once, upstream: standardize formats, dedupe, reject or quarantine bad rows.",
        "Principle: the classification drives storage, tooling, and analysis choices."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "customers(customer_id, name)\norders(order_id, customer_id, total)\n-- need: name + SUM(total) per customer",
        "walkthrough": "A JOIN combines related rows via the key they share; GROUP BY with SUM then rolls orders up per customer. A join without a condition creates a Cartesian product \u2014 every customer times every order \u2014 which is never what you want."
      },
