    {
      "domain_id": "cloud-arch",
      "title": "CompTIA Infrastructure (Network+/Server+ Track) \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "IP addressing is hierarchical: the subnet mask splits every address into network and host portions. Two hosts communicate directly only when they share a network \u2014 otherwise traffic goes through the gateway.",
        "Subnet math is powers of two: borrowing n bits creates 2^n subnets with 2^(32-n) - 2 usable hosts each. A /26 carved from a /24 yields four subnets of 62 usable hosts.",
        "Switches versus routers: switches forward within a broadcast domain using MAC tables; routers separate broadcast domains and forward between networks using IP routing tables. VLANs need a router (or layer-3 switch) to talk to each other.",
        "DNS and DHCP are the invisible infrastructure: DHCP automates addressing (Discover, Offer, Request, Acknowledge); DNS translates names. When the internet is 'down' but IPs ping, suspect DNS.",
        "Virtualization abstracts hardware: a type-1 hypervisor runs on bare metal for production servers; type-2 runs atop an OS for labs and desktops. VMs share physical NICs and storage through virtual switches and datastores.",
        "Storage protocols fit workloads: block storage (iSCSI, Fibre Channel) for databases and VM datastores needing raw disks; file storage (SMB, NFS) for shared files; object storage for scale-out unstructured data."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "shell",
        "code": "ip -brief addr show\nip route show default",
        "walkthrough": "The first command shows each interface's IP address and mask; the second shows the default gateway. If the address and mask are right but the gateway is missing or wrong, the LAN works and the internet does not."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: assigning the network or broadcast address to a host \u2014 for example .0 or .255 in a /24.",
        "Common trap: confusing a switch with a router \u2014 adding another switch never fixes an inter-VLAN routing problem.",
        "Common trap: overlapping subnets on VPN tunnels \u2014 identical ranges on both sides break routing.",
        "Common trap: forgetting DNS \u2014 'can't reach the site by name but the IP works' is a DNS problem, not a routing problem."
      ],
      "try_it": "Work through this in the Labs tab.",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "comptia-infra",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/comptia-infra"
    },
