  "resources": {
    "cheatsheet": {
      "title": "CompTIA Infra: Cloud+ / Linux+ / Server+ / Data+ \u2014 Cheat Sheet",
      "sections": [
        {
          "heading": "Cloud Architecture",
          "items": [
            "Shared responsibility splits at the hypervisor for IaaS \u2014 the provider secures facilities, hardware, and virtualization; you own the OS, middleware, apps, and data.",
            "IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS \u2014 the line moves up the stack with PaaS and SaaS, but IaaS guests are always the customer's job.",
            "Auto-scaling \u2014 Auto-scaling matches capacity to demand automatically, and the load balancer spreads traffic across the fleet.",
            "Storage types \u2014 object storage is built for massive numbers of flat, immutable blobs (images, backups) with HTTP access; databases need low-latency block devices that look like local disks.",
            "Common trap \u2014 shared means neither side.",
            "Common trap \u2014 refactor to cloud-native first, deadline or not.",
            "Common trap \u2014 pay the bill and hope next month is better.",
            "Common trap \u2014 buy a bigger discount plan and change nothing.",
            "Read \u2014 Read the setup and name the principle at play before looking at the reasoning. Then compare your answer with the takeaway \u2014 if they differ, re-read the concepts above and try the drill scenarios for this domain."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Linux Administration",
          "items": [
            "Principle \u2014 600 gives read/write to the owner only; the web server runs as a different user and needs read on files plus read+execute on directories to traverse them.",
            "Common trap \u2014 just change the SSH port and call it done.",
            "Common trap \u2014 use longer passwords and keep password auth.",
            "Common trap \u2014 disable GPG checking to make installs faster.",
            "Common trap \u2014 set everything to 777 so nothing is ever blocked.",
            "Key point \u2014 600 gives read/write to the owner only; the web server runs as a different user and needs read on files plus read+execute on directories to traverse them. 644/755 is the standard; 777 hands write access to everyone \u2014 a vulnerability, not a fix."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Server Hardware",
          "items": [
            "RAID \u2014 RAID 0 has no redundancy, and RAID 5's parity rebuilds on large disks are slow and risky \u2014 RAID 10 is the standard answer for performance + redundancy.",
            "ECC RAM corrects single-bit errors in hardware and logs them \u2014 which is why servers use it and desktops usually do not. POST memory errors localize the fault to a slot or stick.",
            "Hardware diagnostics \u2014 baseboard management controllers run independently of the host OS: they give KVM console, power cycle, and hardware sensors even when the OS is dead.",
            "Power redundancy \u2014 redundant hot-swappable PSUs exist so a failure is a maintenance event, not an outage \u2014 but redundancy is now gone, so the next failure is fatal.",
            "Common trap \u2014 power the server down first to be safe.",
            "Common trap \u2014 ask a non-technical employee to pull the plug.",
            "Common trap \u2014 ignore it; random crashes are normal.",
            "Common trap \u2014 open all the rack doors permanently."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Virtualization",
          "items": [
            "Snapshots vs backups \u2014 real backups are independent, restorable copies \u2014 ideally offsite and tested.",
            "Data protection \u2014 type 1 hypervisors (ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM) sit directly on hardware: lower overhead, better isolation, built for data centers.",
            "Containers vs VMs \u2014 live migration moves running VMs between hosts with no downtime, letting you evacuate a host for maintenance. Containers package the app with its dependencies but share the host OS kernel \u2014 no guest OS per instance, no hypervisor boot.",
            "Common trap \u2014 containers are just faster VMs with their own kernels.",
            "Common trap \u2014 they should delete old snapshots to save space and call it done.",
            "Common trap \u2014 snapshots are a complete backup strategy.",
            "Common trap \u2014 patch the host with VMs running on it."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Data Concepts",
          "items": [
            "Data types \u2014 structured data fits relational tables; semi-structured (JSON logs) has tags but no fixed schema; unstructured (images, video) has no predefined model.",
            "Data quality \u2014 the transform stage exists to enforce quality once, upstream: standardize formats, dedupe, reject or quarantine bad rows.",
            "Principle \u2014 the classification drives storage, tooling, and analysis choices.",
            "Common trap \u2014 a raw data table with no chart.",
            "Common trap \u2014 everything is big data regardless of form.",
            "Common trap \u2014 give analysts raw production data.",
            "Common trap \u2014 run two separate queries and merge them by hand in a spreadsheet.",
            "A JOIN \u2014 A JOIN combines related rows via the key they share; GROUP BY with SUM then rolls orders up per customer. A join without a condition creates a Cartesian product \u2014 every customer times every order \u2014 which is never what you want."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Operations & Security",
          "items": [
            "Backups \u2014 RPO is the maximum tolerable data loss: a 4-hour RPO needs backups at least every 4 hours.",
            "RPO \u2014 document every rule's owner and purpose.",
            "Change control \u2014 firewall changes alter the security boundary: they need the same change control as any production change \u2014 justification, approval, testing, and a backout plan.",
            "Alert fatigue is a tuning failure \u2014 when everything is critical, nothing is.",
            "Common trap \u2014 trust that backups work without testing.",
            "Common trap \u2014 disable monitoring to stop the noise.",
            "Common trap \u2014 email the firewall admin after adding it.",
            "Common trap \u2014 freeze all firewall changes forever."
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "glossary": [
      {
        "term": "OS",
        "def": "Hardware diagnostics: baseboard management controllers run independently of the host OS: they give KVM console, power cycle, and hardware sensors even when the OS is dead."
      },
      {
        "term": "RPO",
        "def": "Backups: RPO is the maximum tolerable data loss: a 4-hour RPO needs backups at least every 4 hours."
      },
      {
        "term": "KVM",
        "def": "Type 1 hypervisors (ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM) sit directly on hardware: lower overhead, better isolation, built for data centers. Type 2 (VirtualBox, Workstation) rides on a host OS \u2014 fine for labs, wrong for production clusters."
      },
      {
        "term": "VMs",
        "def": "Containers vs VMs: live migration moves running VMs between hosts with no downtime, letting you evacuate a host for maintenance. Containers package the app with its dependencies but share the host OS kernel \u2014 no guest OS per instance, no\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "RAID",
        "def": "RAID 0 has no redundancy, and RAID 5's parity rebuilds on large disks are slow and risky \u2014 RAID 10 is the standard answer for performance + redundancy."
      },
      {
        "term": "Data",
        "def": "Hybrid keeps sensitive data where the law demands while using public cloud for scale where it is allowed. Data residency and sovereignty requirements are hard constraints that architecture must satisfy, not risks to accept."
      },
      {
        "term": "RAM",
        "def": "POST memory errors localize the fault to a slot or stick. Reseating fixes contact issues; single-stick testing isolates the failure; replace only the bad DIMM."
      },
      {
        "term": "VM",
        "def": "CPU ready time measures how long a VM waits for a physical core: over-allocated vCPUs make scheduling harder, not faster. Adding vCPUs to contended hosts worsens it."
      },
      {
        "term": "IaaS",
        "def": "Shared responsibility splits at the hypervisor for IaaS: the provider secures facilities, hardware, and virtualization; you own the OS, middleware, apps, and data. The line moves up the stack with PaaS and SaaS, but IaaS guests are alway\u2026"
      },
      {
        "term": "DIMM",
        "def": "POST memory errors localize the fault to a slot or stick. Reseating fixes contact issues; single-stick testing isolates the failure; replace only the bad DIMM."
      },
      {
        "term": "SaaS",
        "def": "IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS: the line moves up the stack with PaaS and SaaS, but IaaS guests are always the customer's job."
      },
      {
        "term": "PaaS",
        "def": "IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS: the line moves up the stack with PaaS and SaaS, but IaaS guests are always the customer's job."
      },
      {
        "term": "PSUs",
        "def": "Redundant hot-swappable PSUs exist so a failure is a maintenance event, not an outage \u2014 but redundancy is now gone, so the next failure is fatal. Replace promptly during a planned moment, and confirm the PSUs are on separate circuits."
      },
      {
        "term": "Alert",
        "def": "Alert fatigue is a tuning failure: when everything is critical, nothing is."
      },
      {
        "term": "Cloud",
        "def": "Cloud waste is an operational discipline problem: tagging establishes ownership for chargeback, rightsizing matches instances to actual use, and schedules kill idle dev spend. FinOps basics beat provider-hopping every time."
      },
      {
        "term": "Principle",
        "def": "Principle: 600 gives read/write to the owner only; the web server runs as a different user and needs read on files plus read+execute on directories to traverse them."
      },
      {
        "term": "BMC",
        "def": "Baseboard management controllers run independently of the host OS: they give KVM console, power cycle, and hardware sensors even when the OS is dead. It is the standard answer for lights-out remote sites \u2014 no BMC means a truck roll."
      },
      {
        "term": "GPG",
        "def": "Common trap: disable GPG checking to make installs faster."
      },
      {
        "term": "ECC",
        "def": "ECC RAM corrects single-bit errors in hardware and logs them \u2014 which is why servers use it and desktops usually do not. POST memory errors localize the fault to a slot or stick."
      },
      {
        "term": "ECC RAM",
        "def": "ECC RAM corrects single-bit errors in hardware and logs them \u2014 which is why servers use it and desktops usually do not. POST memory errors localize the fault to a slot or stick."
      },
      {
        "term": "Hyper-V",
        "def": "Type 1 hypervisors (ESXi, Hyper-V, KVM) sit directly on hardware: lower overhead, better isolation, built for data centers. Type 2 (VirtualBox, Workstation) rides on a host OS \u2014 fine for labs, wrong for production clusters."
      },
      {
        "term": "systemctl",
        "def": "journalctl: systemctl status shows the state and the most recent log lines; journalctl -u gives the full story, usually a config syntax error from yesterday's edit."
      },
      {
        "term": "Auto-scaling",
        "def": "Auto-scaling matches capacity to demand automatically, and the load balancer spreads traffic across the fleet."
      },
      {
        "term": "Backups",
        "def": "Backups: RPO is the maximum tolerable data loss: a 4-hour RPO needs backups at least every 4 hours."
      },
      {
        "term": "Heat",
        "def": "Heat problems are airflow problems first: missing blanking panels let hot exhaust recirculate into intakes, defeating the HVAC. Hot/cold aisle discipline plus sealed racks keeps cooling effective; adding load or opening doors makes it wo\u2026"
      }
    ],
